In article <5035@rhino.qpsx.oz.au>,
Martin Gregory <marting@rhino.qpsx.oz.au> wrote:
>I'd be interested to know how many times you have to start a game
>before you actually make much progress (say, get to the Quest).  When
>you have your experience, does it allow you to rip through the easly
>levels, or do you, like me now, have to try rather a lot of times
>before you get a decent character going?

For me, the distribution is about as follows:
25% of the games: I get killed real quickly (like, within the first five levels)due to stupidity or bad luck.
35% of the games: I move along nicely for a while, get some armor, get to know
scrolls & stuff, spend time dealing with shops & vaults, and then my luck runs
out, I find myself continuously running away from monsters, lose my grip on
the game and eventually perish.
30% of the games: I get nice 'n' far, good armor, good level, sometimes do my
quest, feel like I'm of ascension quality..... and then I find out a new way
to die.  Like having 15 wands explode in my face getting shocked by a trapped
chest.  Or stupidly stoning the wizard who has the book of the dead.
10% of the games: I ascend.
Of course, in game play time, this means only a very small percentage of the
time goes to the quick-kill games.  
